<section id="cognitive-designer" class="page-section" aria-labelledby="cognitive-designer-h">
  <div class="grid">
    <h2 id="cognitive-designer-h">5—What Is a Cognitive Designer</h2>
  </div>

  <div class="grid">
    <div class="col-6">
      <p>
        A cognitive designer is someone who defines the structure of things based on human cognitive
          capacities, either perceptual or conceptual, with the goal of making them
          clear and <em>understandable</em>.
      </p>
      <p>
        The word “designer” has been compartmentalized into different subfields (interaction
          designer, graphic designer, industrial designer, and so on), and when used on its own, it
          conveys the idea of someone who makes things pretty, and that doesn’t convey enough of myself.
      </p>
      <p>
        But I’m against the idea of design as an artistic medium too. Art is about contemplation; design
          must be about action. The aesthetic side of design is secondary, yet it almost always follows
          on its own if a design first fulfills its purpose, and does so properly.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="col-6">
      <p>
        My goal has always been to make things as simple as possible, and I realized that “simple”
          meant “understandable”: something is simple when we can understand it intuitively.
      </p>
      <p>I’m also a web developer, and have started to put more effort into programming recently. The principles that I use on those fields are the same as in design, but calling myself only a "designer" would undercut my work in those non-visual fields which are more about information and processes.</p>
      <p>
        So what other concept could I have chosen that unifies what I do, the visual form and the internal function, and with the qualities
          I have chosen?
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
